# 3. NON-NEGOTIABLE ENGINEERING RULES

1. Inspect before modifying.
2. Preserve existing working functionality.
3. Do not perform an unnecessary full rewrite.
4. Do not remove existing APIs without compatibility or migration planning.
5. Do not replace working security controls with weaker alternatives.
6. Do not introduce hardcoded credentials, tokens, or encryption keys.
7. Do not expose secrets in logs, responses, terminal output, or frontend state.
8. Validate all user-controlled input on the backend.
9. Enforce RBAC on the backend, not only in the UI.
10. Use transactions for multi-step persistent operations.
11. Make long-running operations asynchronous and observable.
12. Implement reliable cleanup and cancellation.
13. Avoid duplicate deployment execution.
14. Never report an operation as successful until its actual result has been verified.
15. Do not fabricate successful tests, deployments, integrations, or infrastructure.
16. Use existing dependencies where appropriate; add dependencies only when justified.
17. Prefer modular architecture and clear interfaces.
18. Maintain Windows, Linux, and macOS compatibility where the existing product supports them.
19. Never expose arbitrary host command execution through an unauthenticated endpoint.
20. Never execute arbitrary user-supplied shell commands without strict authorization and isolation.

When a feature requires unavailable infrastructure, implement a clear adapter boundary, validation, error handling, and an honest unavailable status. Do not simulate a successful real deployment.

